A real symmetric matrix $matbold(H)$ can be diagonalised by an orthogonal transformation. Using coordinates along the principal axes (eigenvectors), in $N$ dimensions
$
  delta x^tp matbold(H) delta x = sum_(i=1)^N lambda_i (delta x_i)^2,
$
so the sign of the quadratic form is determined entirely by the eigenvalues:

- $matbold(H)$ is positive definite iff all eigenvalues satisfy $lambda_i > 0$;

- $matbold(H)$ is negative definite iff all eigenvalues satisfy $lambda_i < 0$;

- if all eigenvalues are non-zero but of mixed signs, the quadratic form takes both signs, and $matbold(H)$ is indefinite;

- if any eigenvalue is zero, the quadratic form does not determine definiteness, and higher order terms in the Taylor series are needed to classify the stationary point.
